 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the workers' compensation law, in relation to the rates of payment for the treatment and care of injured employees",
    "basePrintNo": "S7455",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7455-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §13, Work Comp L",
    "lawSection": "Workers' Compensation Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7455\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the workers' compensation law, in relation to the\nrates of payment for the treatment and care of injured employees and\nproviding for the repeal of such provisions upon expiration thereof\n\n\n PURPOSE :\nChapter 592 of 2006 allowed general hospitals to receive reimbursement\nfor the use if implantable hardware and instrumentation in spinal\nsurgeries for patients eligible under the Workers' Compensations law.\nThe schedule fee (rates) for reimbursement for inpatient hospital\nservices is set by the Commissioner of Health and are case based\npayments per discharge for each diagnosis-related group (DRG).\n\nAs required by statute, the Department of Health provided the Workers'\nCompensation Board with its new reimbursement rates for inpatient\nhospital care on January 4, 2010. These rates are based on a new\nmethodology adopted by DOH know as All Patients Refined Diagnosis\nRelated Groups (APR-DRG). The new rates were effective ford discharges\nbetween December 1, 2009 and December 31, 2009. There will be new\nrates for discharges on or after January 1, 2010. These rates will\nhave different AP-DRG codes.\n\nThis bill will allow for the continued application of WCL§13(a-1)\nthrough March 30, 2011 and will apply to whichever AP-DRG codes are in\neffect for the specific procedures.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\nAmends subdivision (a-1) of section 13 of the workers' compensation\nlaw.\n\n EXISTING LAW :\nChapter 592 of the Laws of 2006, which amended the Workers'\nCompensation Law regarding the reimbursement of general hospitals for\nthe use of implantable hardware and instrumentation in spinal\nsurgeries, was enacted.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nBack injuries are a common problem for injured employees seeking\nbenefits from the Workers' Compensation program.  For many diagnoses,\nspinal fusion surgery is the recommended treatment for such\nindividuals and results in superior outcomes. In most cases, the use\nof spinal implants is the preferable method of performing spinal\nfusion surgery and has become the medical standard for care. From a\nfinancial standpoint, however, performing fusion surgery with implants\ninvolves a higher cost. This higher cost is more than recouped in the\nsavings derived from a shorter hospital stay, more effective\nrehabilitation and better results. The ability of the patient to\nreturn to work sooner presents additional savings for employers and\ninsurers with an employee out with a Worker's Compensation injury or\ndisability.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\nNew Bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nUndetermined, though savings will be achieved by moving people from\nWorkers' Compensation, back into the workforce.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall expire March 31, 2011\nwhen upon such date the provisions of this act shall be deemed\nrepealed.\n",
