    "lawCode": "Amd §13, Work Comp L; amd §3, Chap 592 of 2006",
    "lawSection": "Workers' Compensation Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S7455A\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act to amend the workers' compensation law, in relation to the rates\nof payment for the treatment and care of injured employees; and to amend\nchapter 592 of the laws of 2006 amending the workers' compensation law\nrelating to the rates of payment for the treatment and care of injured\nemployees, in relation to the effectiveness thereof\n\nPURPOSE:\nChapter 592 of 2006 allowed general hospitals to receive reimbursement\nfor the use if implantable hardware and instrumentation in spinal\nsurgeries for patients eligible under the Workers' Compensation law. The\nschedule fee (rates) for reimbursement for inpatient hospital services\nis set by the Commissioner of Health and are case based payments per\ndischarge for each diagnosis-related group (DRG).\n\nAs required by statute, the Department of Health provided the Workers'\nCompensation Board with its new reimbursement rates for inpatient hospi-\ntal care on January 4,2010. These rates are based on a new methodology\nadopted by DOH know as All Patients Refined Diagnosis Related Groups\n(APR-DRG). The new rates were effective for all discharges between\nDecember 1,2009 and December 31, 2009. There will be new rates for\ndischarges on or after January 1,2010. These rates will have different\nAP-DRG codes.\n\nThis bill will allow for the continued application of WCL§ 13 (a-l)\nthrough March 30, 2011 and will apply to whichever APR-DRG codes are in\neffect for the specific procedures.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\nAmends subdivision (a-1) of section 13 of the workers' compensation law.\n\nEXISTING LAW:\nChapter 592 of the laws of 2006, which amended the Workers' Compensation\nlaw regarding the reimbursement of general hospitals for the use of\nimplantable hardware and instrumentation in spinal surgeries, was\nenacted.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nBack injuries are a common problem for injured employees seeking bene-\nfits from the Workers' Compensation program.  For many diagnoses, spinal\nfusion surgery is the recommended treatment for such individuals and\nresults in superior outcomes. In most cases, the use of spinal Implants\nis the preferable method of performing spinal fusion surgery and has\nbecome the medical standard for care.  From a financial standpoint,\nhowever, performing fusion surgery with implants involves a higher cost.\nThis higher cost is more than recouped in the savings derived from a\nshorter hospital stay, more effective rehabilitation and better results.\nThe ability of the patient to return to work sooner presents additional\n\nsavings for employers and insurers with an employee out with a Worker's\nCompensation injury or disability.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nNew Bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nUndetermined, though savings will be achieved by moving people from\nWorkers' Compensation back into the workforce.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall expire March 31, 2011\nwhen upon such date the provisions of this act shall be deemed repealed.\n\n",
